/E (Resource Storage)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 638 0 R
/ActualText (In order to further increase stability, we changed from using two slides to four slides, operating in pairs of two. When one slider was more extended than the other, the storage at the top would tilt, allowing the robot to release the balls.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 639 0 R
/ActualText (This system had the advantage of not having motors at the top, eliminating the issue of motor wires being too short.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 640 0 R
/ActualText <486F77657665722C20746865206D656368616E69736D206D61646520757365206F662052455690732062656172696E6720626C6F636B7320616E6420762D67726F6F76652062656172696E67732C20776869636820636F756C64206E6F7420686F6C6420757020746F20746865207374726573736573206F662074657374696E672074686520726F626F742E20>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 641 0 R
/ActualText (To ensure that the frame remained stable while the two pairs of slides were at different heights, an additional set of sliders made from 420mm extrusions was placed under the frame. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 642 0 R
/ActualText (This version also saw the addition of two front doors, which were chain-driven by two servos. These components were installed on the extrusions above the base, allowing for the individual release of balls. The rudder plate connected the servos through the motor frame, which significantly improved the system's ability to withstand tangential forces. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
