313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 643 0 R
/ActualText (Seeing as we did not have enough chains to power all four slides, we devised a hybrid system, with the first level of the slides powered by chain drives, while the rest of the levels are cascade slides powered by strings. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 644 0 R
/ActualText (This provides stability, as reverse winding is no longer needed for the cascade slides; besides, we also developed a way to fix strings onto the extrusions using parts cut from slider plates.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 645 0 R
/ActualText (Consequently, the strings do not break frequently, reducing maintenance frequency. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
316 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 157 0 R
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 646 0 R
/ActualText (In the fourth version, the design focused on reducing excessive extrusions.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
318 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 647 0 R
/ActualText ( The door servo was replaced with a direct-drive system, and the door's design was revised to a two-part system that passively folds upward, preventing damage to the servo.  )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 648 0 R
/ActualText (Corner brackets were added to increase the torque of the side panels. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 649 0 R
/ActualText (A structure \(below\) providing elasticity to the door is designed to prevent the door servos from breaking under a excessive tangential force)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
