>>
endobj
349 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 656 0 R
/ActualText (For the final version, we decided to change the grabbing structure to two channels with a slight height difference. We place the motors at the front of the chassis and use a chain drive so that the arms of the intake could rotate within a certain range.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 657 0 R
/ActualText <>
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (channels could also produce a deformation in the ball to let it leave the ground, enabling the lower intake structure to carry balls on its own. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Upper Intake Structure)
/E (Upper Intake Structure)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 658 0 R
/ActualText (We also added a structure on top of the intake that scoops the balls upward and carries it securely once the ball is grabbed by the arms at the base. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 659 0 R
/ActualText (One half of the structure consists of a roller with rubber wheels, powered by two servos. The other side uses a piece of tubing to catch the ball, much like a net. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 660 0 R
/ActualText (Both elements together can turn according to an axis, which is also powered by two servos. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
