>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (By separating the robot to several functions and parts, we made a control system that is easier for debugging, reading code, and driving the robot. Specifically, this system creates a clear separation between the command part and hardware part, so we can test each part in order without worrying about their interactions. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
365 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 160 0 R
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
366 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Statemachine Subsystems)
/E (Statemachine Subsystems)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
367 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (We used Statemachine in our Intake and Lift subsystems to simplify the complicated logic of different actions and prevent possible conflict. For example, due to the various height of Nexus Goals, the lift system is designed with 4 lift states. Each state \(ORIGIN, LOW, MID, HIGH\) has predefined actions or motor positions. In addition, we defined the state of intake as HOLDING, RISING and FALLING, accompanied with door state, making the logic intake action clearer.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
368 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Drive Power Rate Limiter)
/E (Drive Power Rate Limiter)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
369 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The rate limiter limits how fast a value can change over time, preventing sudden spikes or drops in input values. In the class of Rate Limiter, we have a timer to calculate the time interval between two samples, and then restrict the increased value by the limits we previously set to make the value vs. time relationship as a linear function. Thus, the acceleration and deceleration of robot due to sudden gamepad movements are limited to a specific range, preventing it from tipping over. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 160 0 R
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
