382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (We have utilized the location of the AprilTags to drive the robot at the desired position. By adding Feeding The Future AprilTag Package into the program, we can access the AprilTagDetection including angle offsets as well as camera distance to the AprilTag. Then, we used two PIDF controllers to drive the chassis to approach the set point to help our lift mechanism to release balls more stably.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The image below is the program structure we designed to implement the field-relative robot localization based on AprilTags. We tested a Kalman Filter to decrease the noise brought by AprilTag ambiguity so that we could derive field-relative robot pose by merely capturing AprilTag video stream. However, restricted by the low accuracy of REV wheels and the encoders embedded in the chassis motors, we were unable to combine the pose derived from drive-base pose estimator and that from vision pose estimator, so the field-relative robot pose is not precise enough to support more advanced usages. We are optimizing our program to decrease the negative effects brought by the hardware to make the pose estimator more stable.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
384 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 161 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Intake with Touch and Color Sensors)
/E (Intake with Touch and Color Sensors)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Our robot has the ability to intake 1 ball from ground. However, the drivers do not have a good view of the front of the robot. For this reason, we decided to add 2 color sensors on each side of the intake. When the color sensors detect a ball that has color, the intake will close automatically. Moreover, 2 touch sensors are used to prevent collision between the intake system and lift system, so drivers can control the robot more easily.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
